artifact.self_contained_problem:
Develop a rigorous mathematical and methodological framework for unifying mathematical biology in the following sense. Suppose one is given:

(i) A collection $\{D_1, D_2, \dots, D_n\}$ of heterogeneous biological datasets, where each $D_i$ may differ from the others in measurement modality, scale (molecular, cellular, tissue, organismal, population), resolution, noise structure, and underlying sample space.

(ii) A collection $\{M_1, M_2, \dots, M_m\}$ of mathematical models drawn from disparate formalisms (e.g. systems of ordinary or partial differential equations, stochastic processes, agent-based models, discrete dynamical systems, network models, statistical models), each intended to describe some biological phenomenon, together with a set of competing hypotheses $\{H_1, \dots, H_k\}$ that these models instantiate.

Construct a formal framework — for instance, a category, a meta-model, a common state-space embedding, or a model-comparison theory — together with explicit procedures, such that:

(a) Any two models $M_i$ and $M_j$ can be compared and one can decide in a principled, quantitative way whether they are conceptually equivalent, one is a limit/coarse-graining of the other, or they are genuinely distinct;

(b) A given hypothesis $H_\ell$ can be tested simultaneously against the entire collection $\{D_1, \dots, D_n\}$, producing a coherent measure of consistency that integrates evidence across the heterogeneous datasets;

(c) Multiple competing hypotheses can be ranked against the combined evidence in a way that does not depend on the particular formalism in which each $M_i$ is expressed;

(d) The framework supports the construction of integrated theories spanning multiple biological scales and subfields, rather than producing only subfield-specific descriptions.

Provide such a framework, prove that it satisfies (a)–(d) in a precise mathematical sense, and demonstrate its applicability on at least one nontrivial example involving models from different formalisms and datasets from different scales.

